"""phone-gesture-test: the quick-settings swipe gestures, run on the phone and
checked through the shell's own state. Touch points come from the output and
the page's own window, so no number in it is one device's.

usage: tools/phone-gesture-test [--record] [--keep-tiles] [--loud] [--out DIR] [--list] [--cleanup] [ID ...]

Run it on the phone, in the user's session, from a checkout (or with
tools/phone-gesture-touch next to it). Each test sets up a known screen
itself, so the result doesn't depend on the device, its screen size or the
user's setup:
  - pages and the sheet open through the shell's IPC (openWifi, openBluetooth,
    summon), never by a tap on a tile, so the tile layout doesn't matter;
  - for the run, quick settings use the package's default tile layout (the
    user's ~/.config/omamobile/quicksettings.json is put back afterwards);
  - "home" is an empty workspace, "app" one with a terminal the script opens
    and closes again;
  - the shade's tests post fictional notifications within the history's room
    and dismiss them again; do-not-disturb is on during the run;
  - only the gesture under test is a real touch, through a virtual
    touchscreen (tools/phone-gesture-touch, root), at points taken from the
    output's size and the page's own window (hyprctl layers), in logical
    pixels.
Verdicts are computed, never judged: a test passes when the shell's state
after the gesture is the one the scenario expects (`omarchy-shell shell call
omamobile.quicksettings state`) and Recents stayed shut, and a page that
should stay open must also show in the pixels that its content moved up with
the drag (docs/GESTURE-TESTS.md).

It takes over the screen for a few minutes (workspaces switch, a terminal
opens, notifications appear and go): tell whoever holds the phone first.

root: phone-gesture-touch needs /dev/uinput. Without write access the script
first gets a sudo ticket (sudo -v, which asks once) and then starts it with
sudo -n, so the helper's stdin only ever carries swipes. GESTURE_TEST_SUDO_PW
supplies the password for an unattended run: it is read once and taken out of
the environment, so no child sees it (the terminal under the pages keeps its
environment for its lifetime), and nothing is stored.

A run that ends early (Ctrl-C, SIGTERM, or SIGHUP when the SSH session or the
USB link drops) puts everything back before it exits. What it is about to
change is written to ~/.local/state/phone-gesture-test/run.json first, and
the file goes once everything is back, so what a run that couldn't clean up
left (SIGKILL, a crash, a power cut) is put back at the next start, or by
--cleanup alone.
"""
